 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>A big shrimp is too long for one bite and for a spoonful of poke.</li><li>Cut across the middle, it gives two short, chunky, bite-size pieces that still look like shrimp, cook evenly in a couple of minutes and mix through the mayo instead of tangling.</li><li>The two cut ends pick up a little extra soy glaze and mayo.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why cut them in half crosswise"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>A splash of soy — not a sticky sauce — and a quick sear keep the shrimp pink rather than brown.</li><li>White Kewpie with about a quarter as much red sriracha is pale coral-pink; with pink shrimp and pink ginger folded through, the whole bowl comes out one cool pink.</li><li>More sriracha turns it orange.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keeping it pink"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>About 4 : 1 mayo to sriracha is mild-medium; go to 3 : 1 for more heat.</li><li>It’s the same sauce as on the <a href=\"tuna-mango-roll.html\">spicy tuna & mango roll</a>.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Spicy mayo ratio"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Hot shrimp make the mayo oily and runny.</li><li>Five minutes spread out on a plate is enough.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Cool before mixing"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>A spoon of cold ikura sits on top, in the middle of the bowl.</li><li>Folding it into the spicy mayo mashes the pearls and turns the mix orange.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Ikura in the center"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The bowl and the cut are yours.</li><li>The frying is Omnivore’s Cookbook’s soy-glazed pan-fry (hot pan, shrimp seared on both sides, sauce in at the end until it coats), cut down to soy alone with a little mirin — no honey or garlic, to keep it pink.</li><li>The ikura in the center, the timings, the cooling and all the amounts are this site’s starting points.</li><li>Adjust to taste.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Assumed: what's the source and what's mine"
  }
 ],
